(window.SERVICE_BANKS = window.SERVICE_BANKS || {})["sqlproj"] = {"name":"SQL Projects & SqlPackage","icon":"📦","blurb":"SQL Database Projects, SqlPackage and CI/CD from A to Z: SDK-style projects, building and validating dacpacs, code analysis, pre/post-deployment scripts, SQLCMD variables, references, schema compare, drift, every SqlPackage action, publish profiles, and GitHub Actions deployments.","order":3,"sourceNote":"Written from the Microsoft Learn documentation; every explanation links the page it was checked against.","sections":[{"id":"1","name":"SQL database projects"},{"id":"2","name":"Creating, building and validating"},{"id":"3","name":"Project contents"},{"id":"4","name":"Schema compare and drift"},{"id":"5","name":"SqlPackage"},{"id":"6","name":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions"}],"questions":[{"id":"sqlproj-1.1-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.1 · Declarative projects and the .dacpac","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about SQL database projects.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SQL projects are **declarative**: the project describes the desired state, and the build validates references and syntax, then outputs a `.dacpac`. Publishing compares the `.dacpac` with the target and generates the steps, creating objects in dependency order for new databases. One `.dacpac` can update a whole fleet of databases. The engine is the DacFx .NET library.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **One `CREATE` per object:** `Tables/Customer.sql` holds `CREATE TABLE dbo.Customer (Id int PRIMARY KEY, Name nvarchar(100) NOT NULL);`. To add an email column, you add a line to that same `CREATE TABLE`. You never write an `ALTER`.\n- **Build → `.dacpac`:** `dotnet build` writes `bin/Debug/Db.dacpac`, a zip file with the whole schema model.\n- **Publish applies only the differences:** against a database that already has `Customer` without `Email`, the plan has a single step, `ALTER TABLE [dbo].[Customer] ADD [Email] NVARCHAR (256) NULL;`. Against an empty server, it creates everything in dependency order.\n- **Hand-written `ALTER` scripts** is false. DacFx works them out at deployment time, for whatever state each target is in.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Each object is declared once with a `CREATE` statement; to change it, you edit that one file.","Building the project produces a `.dacpac` file.","Publishing a `.dacpac` to an existing database computes the differences and applies only the needed changes.","You must write `ALTER` scripts for every change by hand."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-1.1-2","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.1 · Declarative projects and the .dacpac","scenario":"","stem":"A view in your project references a column that doesn't exist in any table in the project. When is this caught?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The build validates the relationships between objects (a view can't use a table or column that isn't in the project) and checks syntax against the **target platform**. Errors fail the build; warnings, such as inconsistent casing, don't.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **At build:** `dotnet build` fails with an error like *SQL71501: View: [dbo].[vCustomers] has an unresolved reference to object [dbo].[Customer].[Phone]*, before anything is deployed.\n- **Only in production:** that's what happens with loose scripts run straight against a database. A project catches it on the developer's machine or in CI.\n- **Never** is false: reference validation is one of the main reasons to use a project.\n- **Only by SqlPackage Extract:** Extract *reads* a live database into a `.dacpac`, and doesn't validate your project's files at all.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"When the project is built, because the build validates references between objects"},{"key":"B","text":"Only when the view is executed in production"},{"key":"C","text":"Never; SQL projects don't validate references"},{"key":"D","text":"Only by SqlPackage Extract"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-1.1-3","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.1 · Declarative projects and the .dacpac","scenario":"","stem":"Your app uses EF Core migrations, but the DBA wants the schema tracked as the single source of truth for CI/CD. What do the docs say about SQL projects in this case?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"SQL projects track the source of truth for database state, including when an ORM such as EF Core is used. You can extract any existing database into a project with graphical tools or the command line, whatever created it.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Extract the schema into a project:** in VS Code, **Create Project from Database**, or `sqlpackage /Action:Extract /SourceConnectionString:\"...\" /TargetFile:Db.sqlproj /p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject`. The project then shows the real state of the database, whether EF Core migrations or anything else changed it, and CI can diff it.\n- **Can't be used with any ORM** is false. The project tracks the database, not the code that talks to it.\n- **Delete the EF Core model:** the app still needs its model to query the database. The two can live side by side.\n- **Only new databases** is false. Extracting an existing database is the usual way to start.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"SQL projects can track the database state even with an ORM such as EF Core; you can extract the schema into a project"},{"key":"B","text":"SQL projects can't be used with any ORM"},{"key":"C","text":"You must delete the EF Core model first"},{"key":"D","text":"SQL projects only work for new databases"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-1.2-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.2 · SDK-style versus original projects, and tools","scenario":"","stem":"Which features do **SDK-style** (Microsoft.Build.Sql) projects have that original SQL projects don't? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"SDK-style projects are the future format, used by the VS Code SQL Database Projects extension and SQL database in Fabric source control. They add .NET 8+ cross-platform builds, **package references** and default **globbing**. Visual Studio 2026 supports only the original format; SDK-style is preview in VS 2022. SQLCLR objects still need .NET Framework.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nAn SDK-style project file:\n\n```xml\n<Project DefaultTargets=\"Build\">\n  <Sdk Name=\"Microsoft.Build.Sql\" Version=\"1.0.0\" />\n  <PropertyGroup>\n    <Name>Db</Name>\n    <DSP>Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlAzureV12DatabaseSchemaProvider</DSP>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master\" Version=\"160.2.2\" />\n  </ItemGroup>\n</Project>\n```\n\n- **Cross-platform builds:** `dotnet build` works on an `ubuntu-latest` runner or a Mac. Original projects need Visual Studio's MSBuild on Windows.\n- **Package references:** the `master` reference above comes from NuGet. Original projects reference `.dacpac` files by path.\n- **Globbing:** there's no list of `<Build Include=\"Tables\\Customer.sql\" />` items. Every `.sql` file in the folder is included, so adding a file needs no project edit, and merge conflicts in the `.sqlproj` mostly go away.\n- **Support in Visual Studio 2026's original SSDT designer** is false. That's the original format only; SDK-style projects open in VS Code or the preview SDK-style SSDT in Visual Studio 2022.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects) · [sql projects tools](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-tools)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Cross-platform .NET 8+ builds"},{"key":"B","text":"NuGet package references for database references"},{"key":"C","text":"Default globbing of `.sql` files in the project folder"},{"key":"D","text":"Support in Visual Studio 2026's original SSDT designer"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-1.2-2","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.2 · SDK-style versus original projects, and tools","scenario":"","stem":"You open an original (non-SDK) SQL project with SQLCLR objects. What should you know before converting it to SDK-style?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"SQLCLR is supported in both formats, but it requires .NET Framework, which limits where the project builds. Conversion is done in place: back up the `.sqlproj`, then change it to reference the `Microsoft.Build.Sql` SDK (removing explicit `Build Include` entries thanks to globbing).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Still needs .NET Framework:** after conversion, the SQLCLR assembly still has to be compiled for .NET Framework. A pipeline that builds on `ubuntu-latest` fails, so keep that project on a `windows-latest` runner.\n- **Conversion is impossible** is false. SDK-style projects can hold CLR objects. The limit is where they build.\n- **Conversion removes the CLR objects** is false. The conversion changes the project file in place. Your objects stay, which is why you back up the `.sqlproj` first.\n- **SQLCLR works the same on Linux** is false. .NET Framework is Windows-only, so the CLR part can't build on Linux or macOS.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects) · [sql projects tools](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-tools)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"SQLCLR objects need .NET Framework, so the converted project still has to build on Windows with .NET Framework"},{"key":"B","text":"SDK-style projects can't contain any CLR objects, so conversion is impossible"},{"key":"C","text":"Conversion removes the CLR objects automatically"},{"key":"D","text":"Nothing; SQLCLR works the same on Linux"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-1.2-3","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 1.2 · SDK-style versus original projects, and tools","scenario":"","stem":"Match each tool to its SQL projects support.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"VS Code opens both formats and is GA for SDK-style projects. SSDT in Visual Studio supports original projects; an SDK-style SSDT is in preview for VS 2022. SSMS adds project support through the Database DevOps workload. All of them can create a project from an existing database.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **VS Code (SQL Database Projects extension):** create, build and publish both formats, and convert original projects to SDK-style. Its SDK-style support is GA, and it runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.\n- **SSDT in Visual Studio 2026:** the classic designer (table designer, schema compare, publish dialog), but only for original `.sqlproj` files.\n- **SDK-style SSDT in Visual Studio 2022:** a separate preview that opens SDK-style projects in Visual Studio.\n- **SSMS:** the **Database DevOps** workload in the SSMS installer adds SQL projects, so a DBA can create a project from a database without another tool.\n- **No SQL project support** doesn't fit any of them: all four can at least create a project from an existing database.\n\n**Source:** [sql database projects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects) · [sql projects tools](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-tools)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Visual Studio Code (SQL Database Projects extension)","SSDT in Visual Studio 2026","SDK-style SSDT in Visual Studio 2022","SQL Server Management Studio"],"values":["Database DevOps workload with project support","No SQL project support","Original projects only","SDK-style projects in preview","SDK-style projects, generally available"],"answer":["SDK-style projects, generally available","Original projects only","SDK-style projects in preview","Database DevOps workload with project support"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-2.1-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.1 · Creating and building a project","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the commands that install the tools, create an SDK-style project and build it.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`dotnet new install Microsoft.Build.Sql.Templates` adds the templates; `dotnet new sqlproj -n <name>` creates a project (`-tp` sets the target platform). `dotnet build` validates the project and writes the `.dacpac`, by default to `bin/Debug`. You can also create a project from an existing database in VS Code, Visual Studio or SSMS.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`dotnet tool install`** installs a .NET *tool*, such as `dotnet tool install -g Microsoft.SqlPackage`. Templates use `dotnet new install`.\n- **`dotnet add`** adds a reference to an existing project, for example `dotnet add package Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master` (it can't create a project).\n- **`database`** and **`sqlproject`** aren't template names. `dotnet new list sql` shows the installed one: `sqlproj`.\n- **`dotnet pack`** builds the project into a **NuGet package**, so other projects can use it as a package reference. **`dotnet publish`** doesn't deploy to a database; deployment is SqlPackage's Publish action.\n- **`bacpac`** is a schema-plus-data file from SqlPackage **Export**, never a build output. **`sqlx`** isn't a SQL projects file type.\n\n**Source:** [get started](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/get-started) · [start from existing database](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/tutorials/start-from-existing-database)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"dotnet [[0]] install Microsoft.Build.Sql.Templates\ndotnet new [[1]] -n MyDatabaseProject\ncd MyDatabaseProject\ndotnet [[2]]          # output: bin/Debug/MyDatabaseProject.[[3]]","options":[["add","new","tool"],["database","sqlproj","sqlproject"],["build","pack","publish"],["bacpac","dacpac","sqlx"]],"answer":["new","sqlproj","build","dacpac"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-2.1-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.1 · Creating and building a project","scenario":"","stem":"You want to build the same project for SQL Server 2019 just once, without editing the `.sqlproj`. Which command does that?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"MSBuild properties can be overridden on the command line with `/p:`, so `/p:DSP=...Sql150...` validates against SQL Server 2019 for that build only. (`-tp` sets the target platform when *creating* a project.)\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`/p:DSP=...Sql150...`:** for example, a nightly job runs `dotnet build /p:DSP=Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.Sql150DatabaseSchemaProvider` to prove the schema still works on SQL Server 2019, while the `.sqlproj` keeps targeting Azure SQL Database.\n- **`dotnet build --target sql2019`:** `--target` (`-t`) picks an MSBuild *target*, such as `Build` or `Clean`. `sql2019` isn't one, so the build fails.\n- **`sqlpackage /Action:Build`:** SqlPackage has no Build action. Its actions are Extract, Publish, Export, Import, DeployReport, DriftReport and Script.\n- **`dotnet new sqlproj -tp ...`** creates a *new* project with that target platform. It doesn't build your existing one.\n\n**Source:** [get started](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/get-started) · [start from existing database](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/tutorials/start-from-existing-database)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`dotnet build /p:DSP=Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.Sql150DatabaseSchemaProvider`"},{"key":"B","text":"`dotnet build --target sql2019`"},{"key":"C","text":"`sqlpackage /Action:Build /p:Version=2019`"},{"key":"D","text":"`dotnet new sqlproj -tp Sql150`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-2.2-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.2 · Target platform and project properties","scenario":"","stem":"Match each target platform to its `DSP` value.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The `<DSP>` property sets the target platform, which decides which syntax and functions are valid. Sql160 is SQL Server 2022 and Sql170 is SQL Server 2025; `SqlDwDatabaseSchemaProvider` is an Azure Synapse SQL pool. Override it for one build with `dotnet build /p:DSP=<value>`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **SqlAzureV12:** Azure SQL Database. The build rejects syntax Azure SQL doesn't have, such as `CREATE ENDPOINT` or a `USE` statement.\n- **Sql170:** SQL Server 2025, so 2025-only T-SQL such as `REGEXP_LIKE(Email, '^[^@]+@[^@]+$')` or the `VECTOR` type builds. The same code fails to build with **Sql160**.\n- **SqlDbFabric:** SQL database in Fabric. That's the value in projects that Fabric's git integration commits.\n- **SqlDwUnified:** Fabric Data Warehouse. Its T-SQL surface area differs from SQL Database's, so the build checks the project against the warehouse's rules.\n- **Sql160** (the extra tile) is **SQL Server 2022**, none of the four platforms in this question.\n\n**Source:** [target platform](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/target-platform) · [project properties](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/project-properties)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Azure SQL Database","SQL Server 2025","SQL database in Fabric","Fabric Data Warehouse"],"values":["Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.Sql160DatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.Sql170DatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlAzureV12DatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlDbFabricDatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlDwUnifiedDatabaseSchemaProvider"],"answer":["Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlAzureV12DatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.Sql170DatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlDbFabricDatabaseSchemaProvider","Microsoft.Data.Tools.Schema.Sql.SqlDwUnifiedDatabaseSchemaProvider"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-2.2-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.2 · Target platform and project properties","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about project properties in the `.sqlproj` file.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Many `ALTER DATABASE SET` options map to properties (for example `AllowSnapshotIsolation`, `AutoUpdateStatistics`, `IsChangeTrackingOn`, `CompatibilityMode`), and publish can apply them (see `ScriptDatabaseOptions`). Properties are plain MSBuild XML, so you can make them conditional per configuration.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Database options as properties:**\n\n  ```xml\n  <PropertyGroup>\n    <AllowSnapshotIsolation>True</AllowSnapshotIsolation>\n    <CompatibilityMode>160</CompatibilityMode>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  ```\n\n  Publish then runs `ALTER DATABASE ... SET ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION ON` if the target differs (unless `ScriptDatabaseOptions=False`).\n- **Release-only warnings as errors:**\n\n  ```xml\n  <PropertyGroup Condition=\"'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'\">\n    <TreatTSqlWarningsAsErrors>True</TreatTSqlWarningsAsErrors>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  ```\n\n  `dotnet build` (Debug) warns, and `dotnet build -c Release` in CI fails on the same warning.\n- **The `<Sdk>` element:** changing `Version=\"1.0.0\"` to a newer version upgrades the SDK the project builds with.\n- **Can't be changed after creation** is false. The `.sqlproj` is a plain XML file that you can edit at any time, by hand or in a tool's project properties.\n\n**Source:** [target platform](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/target-platform) · [project properties](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/project-properties)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Database options such as `AllowSnapshotIsolation` or `CompatibilityMode` can be set as project properties.","`TreatTSqlWarningsAsErrors` can be set for the Release configuration only, with a conditional `PropertyGroup`.","An SDK-style project references the SDK with `<Sdk Name=\"Microsoft.Build.Sql\" Version=\"...\" />`.","Project properties can't be changed after the project is created."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-2.3-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.3 · Code analysis","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the project file so code analysis runs on build, rule SR0006 is disabled, and SR0008 is treated as an error.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`RunSqlCodeAnalysis` turns analysis on during build. In `SqlCodeAnalysisRules`, **`-`** disables a rule and **`+!`** makes it an error. Rules include SR0001 (avoid `SELECT *`), SR0008 (use `SCOPE_IDENTITY` instead of `@@IDENTITY`) and SR0010 (deprecated join syntax). Suppress single findings with `StaticCodeAnalysis.SuppressMessages.xml`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`Enabled`** and **`OnBuild`** aren't values. `RunSqlCodeAnalysis` is a boolean, so it takes `True` or `False`.\n- **`!`** and **`~`** aren't rule prefixes, and **`+`** alone doesn't make a rule an error. Only two prefixes matter here: `-` disables a rule and `+!` turns its warning into a build error.\n\nWith this file, a procedure that uses `SELECT @@IDENTITY` fails the build with SR0008 (use `SCOPE_IDENTITY()`, because `@@IDENTITY` can return an id from a trigger's insert). `WHERE Price * 1.2 > 100`, which SR0006 would flag because the column sits inside an expression (so an index on `Price` can't be used), passes silently.\n\n**Source:** [sql code analysis](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/sql-code-analysis/sql-code-analysis)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"<PropertyGroup>\n  <RunSqlCodeAnalysis>[[0]]</RunSqlCodeAnalysis>\n  <SqlCodeAnalysisRules>[[1]]Microsoft.Rules.Data.SR0006;[[2]]Microsoft.Rules.Data.SR0008</SqlCodeAnalysisRules>\n</PropertyGroup>","options":[["Enabled","OnBuild","True"],["!","-","~"],["!","+","+!"]],"answer":["True","-","+!"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-2.3-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 2.3 · Code analysis","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about code analysis.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Rules report warnings unless you mark them as errors (`+!`) or set `TreatTSqlWarningsAsErrors`. Categories are design, naming and performance issues, and you can author custom rules.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Warnings fail the build by default** is false. A `SELECT *` in a view gives *SR0001* as a warning, and the build still succeeds and produces the `.dacpac`.\n- **Suppressing for one file:** a legacy `Procs/LegacyReport.sql` that has to keep `SELECT *` can be listed with rule SR0001 in `StaticCodeAnalysis.SuppressMessages.xml`. The rule keeps applying to every other file.\n- **Custom rules:** with DacFx, you write a .NET class that derives from `SqlCodeAnalysisRule`, such as one that flags tables without a primary key, and ship it as a NuGet package the project references.\n- **SR0001:** `CREATE VIEW dbo.vOrders AS SELECT * FROM dbo.Orders;` triggers it, because the view's columns would change silently if `Orders` changed.\n\n**Source:** [sql code analysis](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/sql-code-analysis/sql-code-analysis)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Code analysis warnings fail the build by default.","`StaticCodeAnalysis.SuppressMessages.xml` can suppress a rule for a specific file.","You can write custom code analysis rules with DacFx extensibility.","SR0001 warns about `SELECT *` in procedures, views and table-valued functions."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-3.1-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 3.1 · Pre- and post-deployment scripts","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about pre- and post-deployment scripts.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Pre/post-deployment scripts are packaged in the `.dacpac` but **not compiled into or validated against the model**, so they should be idempotent (safe to run every deployment). Typical uses: reference data in post-deployment, preparation in pre-deployment.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **One of each:** a project has at most one `<PreDeploy>` and one `<PostDeploy>` script.\n- **The items:**\n\n  ```xml\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <PreDeploy Include=\"Scripts\\Script.PreDeployment.sql\" />\n    <PostDeploy Include=\"Scripts\\Script.PostDeployment.sql\" />\n  </ItemGroup>\n  ```\n\n- **Validated against the model** is false. `INSERT INTO dbo.Contries ...` (a typo) builds fine and only fails when the publish runs it.\n- **`:r` for several files:** `Script.PostDeployment.sql` can just be\n\n  ```sql\n  :r .\\ReferenceData\\Countries.sql\n  :r .\\ReferenceData\\OrderStatus.sql\n  ```\n\n  and the build inlines them in that order.\n\n**Source:** [pre post deployment scripts](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/pre-post-deployment-scripts)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A project can have one pre-deployment script and one post-deployment script.","They're added with `<PreDeploy Include=\"...\" />` and `<PostDeploy Include=\"...\" />`.","They're validated against the database model like other objects.","To run several files, the script can call each one in order with SQLCMD syntax (`:r`)."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-3.1-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 3.1 · Pre- and post-deployment scripts","scenario":"","stem":"Your post-deployment script inserts reference rows and runs on every deployment. How should you write it?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Pre/post-deployment scripts run on **every** publish and aren't validated against the model, so make them safe to rerun. Use SQLCMD variables in them to vary behaviour per environment.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Idempotent `MERGE`:**\n\n  ```sql\n  MERGE dbo.OrderStatus AS t\n  USING (VALUES (1, N'New'), (2, N'Shipped'), (3, N'Cancelled')) AS s (Id, Name)\n     ON t.Id = s.Id\n  WHEN MATCHED AND t.Name <> s.Name THEN UPDATE SET Name = s.Name\n  WHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET THEN INSERT (Id, Name) VALUES (s.Id, s.Name);\n  ```\n\n  The tenth run leaves the same three rows as the first.\n- **`CREATE TABLE`** belongs in the project as an object, so DacFx manages it. A `CREATE TABLE` in a post-deployment script fails on the second publish (*There is already an object named...*).\n- **Fail if rows exist:** that makes every deployment after the first one fail.\n- **It runs only once** is false. Pre- and post-deployment scripts run on every publish.\n\n**Source:** [pre post deployment scripts](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/pre-post-deployment-scripts)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Idempotently, for example `MERGE` or `IF NOT EXISTS ... INSERT`, so repeated runs don't duplicate rows"},{"key":"B","text":"With `CREATE TABLE` statements"},{"key":"C","text":"So it fails if the rows exist, to signal a redeploy"},{"key":"D","text":"It doesn't matter, because it runs only once"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-3.2-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 3.2 · SQLCMD variables","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the SQLCMD variable definition and its use, then set its value at deployment.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SQLCMD variables are tokens written as `$(Name)` and replaced at deployment. Pass values with **`/v:Name=value`** (or in a publish profile). The `DefaultValue` only pre-fills graphical publish dialogs; it isn't compiled into the `.dacpac`, so command-line deployments must supply a value.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`$EnvironmentName`** is PowerShell-style syntax. SQLCMD only replaces `$(EnvironmentName)`, with the parentheses, so `'$EnvironmentName'` stays a literal string and the test is never true.\n- **`@EnvironmentName`** is a T-SQL variable. Nobody declared it, so the script fails with *Must declare the scalar variable \"@EnvironmentName\"*.\n- **`/p:EnvironmentName=production`:** `/p:` sets *deployment properties* such as `BlockOnPossibleDataLoss`. SqlPackage rejects an unknown property name. SQLCMD variables use `/v:`.\n\nAt deployment, `/v:EnvironmentName=production` turns the line into `IF 'production' = 'testing' PRINT 'test data';`, so nothing prints.\n\n**Source:** [sqlcmd variables](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/sqlcmd-variables)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"<ItemGroup>\n  <SqlCmdVariable Include=\"[[0]]\">\n    <DefaultValue>testing</DefaultValue>\n    <Value>$(SqlCmdVar__1)</Value>\n  </SqlCmdVariable>\n</ItemGroup>\n\n-- in a post-deployment script\nIF '[[1]]' = 'testing' PRINT 'test data';\n\nsqlpackage /Action:Publish /SourceFile:Db.dacpac /TargetConnectionString:\"...\" [[2]]","values":["$(EnvironmentName)","$EnvironmentName","/p:EnvironmentName=production","/v:EnvironmentName=production","@EnvironmentName","EnvironmentName"],"answer":["EnvironmentName","$(EnvironmentName)","/v:EnvironmentName=production"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-3.3-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 3.3 · Database, package and system references","scenario":"","stem":"Your project's views reference `sys.objects` and a table in the `Warehouse` database on the same server. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"System objects aren't in the model by default, so add `master` (or `msdb`) as a reference. Cross-database references use a `DatabaseSqlCmdVariable` so the name can change per environment. **Package references** are recommended for new SDK-style projects (original projects can't reference NuGet packages). Same-database references don't need three-part names.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nIn the project file:\n\n```xml\n<ItemGroup>\n  <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master\" Version=\"160.2.2\" />\n  <ProjectReference Include=\"..\\Warehouse\\Warehouse.sqlproj\">\n    <DatabaseSqlCmdVariable>Warehouse</DatabaseSqlCmdVariable>\n  </ProjectReference>\n</ItemGroup>\n<ItemGroup>\n  <SqlCmdVariable Include=\"Warehouse\"><DefaultValue>Warehouse</DefaultValue></SqlCmdVariable>\n</ItemGroup>\n```\n\n- **`master.dacpac`:** without it, `SELECT name FROM sys.objects` gives an unresolved-reference warning, because system views aren't in your model.\n- **`[$(Warehouse)]`:** in test, `/v:Warehouse=Warehouse_Test` makes the same view read `[Warehouse_Test].[Production].[ProductInventory]`.\n- **Package references:** the `master` line above is a NuGet package, so there's no `.dacpac` path to keep up to date on every machine.\n- **Three-part names in the same database** is false. Write `dbo.Orders`, not `[Db].dbo.Orders`. A hard-coded database name breaks when the database is deployed under another name.\n\n**Source:** [database references](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/database-references) · [package references](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/package-references) · [system objects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/system-objects)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Add a reference to `master.dacpac` so system objects are in the model"},{"key":"B","text":"Reference the other database as `[$(Warehouse)].[Production].[ProductInventory]`, with a database reference that defines the `Warehouse` SQLCMD variable"},{"key":"C","text":"In an SDK-style project, a NuGet package reference is the recommended way to add database references"},{"key":"D","text":"Three-part names are required even for objects in the same database"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-3.4-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 3.4 · Refactoring and the refactor log","scenario":"","stem":"You rename the column `Customer.Phone` to `PhoneNumber` in your project. What stops the deployment from dropping the old column and its data?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Renames made through the IDE's refactor feature (or written manually) are recorded in a **`.refactorlog`**. The deployment plan uses it to emit `sp_rename` or schema transfer operations instead of drop-and-create, and records applied operations in the target so they don't run twice.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **The `.refactorlog`:** with the rename recorded, publish generates\n\n  ```sql\n  EXECUTE sp_rename N'[dbo].[Customer].[Phone]', N'PhoneNumber', N'COLUMN';\n  ```\n\n  and records the operation in the target's `dbo.__RefactorLog` table, so it doesn't run again on the next publish. Without the log, the plan drops `Phone` and adds `PhoneNumber`, and with `BlockOnPossibleDataLoss=True` the publish stops instead of losing data.\n- **`IgnoreColumnOrder=True`** stops table rebuilds when only the *order* of columns differs. It doesn't recognize renames.\n- **A post-deployment script** runs *after* the schema changes, by which time the column and its data are already gone.\n- **Renames always lose data** is false: that's what the refactor log is for.\n\n**Source:** [refactor overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/refactor-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The `.refactorlog` file, which tells the deployment plan it's a rename"},{"key":"B","text":"`/p:IgnoreColumnOrder=True`"},{"key":"C","text":"A post-deployment script"},{"key":"D","text":"Nothing; renames always lose data"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-4.1-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 4.1 · Schema comparison","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about schema compare.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Schema compare uses the same DacFx deployment options (for example ignore whitespace, ignore column order, block on possible data loss) and lets you choose object types. A saved `.scmp` holds the source, target and options, and SqlPackage's Publish and Script actions can even use an `.scmp` as the source.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Any to any:** for example, compare the production database (source) with your project (target) to pull a hotfix someone made directly in production back into source control.\n- **Excluding differences:** clear the checkbox next to a `dbo.TempFix` procedure, and the update leaves it alone.\n- **Apply or script:** **Update** changes the target right away. **Generate Script** gives you the T-SQL for a DBA to review and run.\n- **`.scmp` files:** save `prod-vs-project.scmp` with its source, target and options, so the next comparison is one click away, or run `sqlpackage /Action:Script /SourceFile:prod-vs-project.scmp ...`.\n\n**Source:** [schema comparison](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/schema-comparison)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["You can compare a database, a SQL project or a `.dacpac` with any of the others, in either direction.","You can exclude individual differences before updating the target.","You can apply changes directly or generate a script.","Comparisons can be saved as `.scmp` files."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-4.1-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 4.1 · Schema comparison","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about deployment options used by schema compare and publish.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The options come from DacFx deployment options, the same ones SqlPackage exposes as `/p:` properties.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Ignore column order / whitespace:** with `IgnoreWhitespace`, a procedure whose only change is reindented isn't counted as different. With `IgnoreColumnOrder`, a table whose columns are in a different order isn't rebuilt.\n- **Block on possible data loss:** a change from `nvarchar(200)` to `nvarchar(50)` on a table with rows stops the deployment instead of truncating data.\n- **Object types:** for example, leave out *Users*, *Permissions* and *Role memberships* so each environment keeps its own security setup (`/p:ExcludeObjectTypes=Users;Permissions;RoleMembership`).\n- **Can't exclude any** is false. Both individual differences and whole object types can be excluded.\n\n**Source:** [schema comparison](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/schema-comparison)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Ignore column order and ignore whitespace are available options.","Block on possible data loss is one of the options.","You can choose which object types (tables, permissions, indexes and so on) are compared.","Schema compare always applies every difference; you can't exclude any."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-4.2-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 4.2 · Deploy reports and drift reports","scenario":"","stem":"Match each SqlPackage action to its output.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"DeployReport and Script let you review a deployment before running it (both write to `/OutputPath`). DriftReport works with databases registered as data-tier applications; `BlockWhenDriftDetected` (default true) stops publishing to a registered database whose schema has drifted.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **DeployReport:** `report.xml` with entries such as `<Operation Name=\"Alter\"><Item Value=\"[dbo].[Customer]\" Type=\"SqlTable\" /></Operation>` and any data-loss alerts, which a pipeline can parse to decide whether a human needs to approve.\n- **DriftReport:** for a database registered as a data-tier application, the report lists, for example, an index someone added by hand since registration.\n- **Script:** a `.sql` file with the exact `ALTER`s publish would run, for a DBA to review or run in a change window.\n- **Publish:** the changes are applied to the database.\n- **A `.bacpac` file** is the output of **Export**, which has its own question.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage deploy drift report](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-deploy-drift-report)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["DeployReport","DriftReport","Script","Publish"],"values":["A .bacpac file","A T-SQL script that would update the target","An XML report of changes made to a registered database since it was registered","An XML report of the changes a publish would make","The changes applied to the target database"],"answer":["An XML report of the changes a publish would make","An XML report of changes made to a registered database since it was registered","A T-SQL script that would update the target","The changes applied to the target database"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-4.2-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 4.2 · Deploy reports and drift reports","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the command that writes an XML report of what a publish would change, without changing the database.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**DeployReport** lists the planned changes as XML in `/OutputPath`. Use **Script** (with `/OutputPath` or `/DeployScriptPath`) to get the T-SQL instead, and **DriftReport** to see changes made to a registered database since registration.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`DriftReport`** compares a *registered* database with its registration snapshot. It doesn't take a `.dacpac` source and says nothing about what a publish would change.\n- **`Script`** writes T-SQL instead of XML: `/Action:Script ... /OutputPath:./deploy.sql`.\n- **`ReportFile`** isn't a SqlPackage parameter.\n- **`TargetFile`** is the *output file* of Extract and Export (the `.dacpac` or `.bacpac` they write), not of a report.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage deploy drift report](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-deploy-drift-report)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"sqlpackage /Action:[[0]] \\\n  /SourceFile:Db.dacpac \\\n  /TargetConnectionString:\"$CONN\" \\\n  /[[1]]:./report.xml","options":[["DeployReport","DriftReport","Script"],["OutputPath","ReportFile","TargetFile"]],"answer":["DeployReport","OutputPath"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.1-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.1 · SqlPackage actions and installation","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about SqlPackage.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SqlPackage exposes DacFx: **Extract, Publish, DeployReport, DriftReport, Script** for deployments and **Export, Import** for portability. Files are compressed but **not encrypted**, and a `.bacpac` contains data, so protect them. `SqlPackage /Version` prints the build number, useful in pipeline logs.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`dotnet tool install -g Microsoft.SqlPackage`:** then `sqlpackage /Version` works from any shell, and `dotnet tool update -g Microsoft.SqlPackage` upgrades it.\n- **Cross-platform:** the same command line runs on Windows, a Linux build agent or a Mac.\n- **Encrypted** is false. A `.bacpac` is a zip file: rename `prod.bacpac` to `prod.zip` and you can open `model.xml` and the `Data` folder, which holds the table rows in BCP format. Anyone with the file has the data, so store and transfer it like a backup.\n- **Standalone install for pipelines:** the copy that comes with SSMS or Visual Studio follows *their* update cycle, so a pipeline can't control its version. A standalone install can be pinned and updated on purpose.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage) · [sqlpackage download](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-download)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The recommended install is `dotnet tool install -g Microsoft.SqlPackage`.","SqlPackage runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.","`.bacpac` and `.dacpac` files are encrypted.","For pipelines, a standalone SqlPackage install is recommended over the copy bundled with SSMS or Visual Studio."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.2-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.2 · Publish and its properties","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the command that publishes a `.dacpac` to Azure SQL, blocks on data loss, never drops objects that aren't in the project, and passes an environment variable.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Publish incrementally updates (or creates) a database to match the `.dacpac` (`/SourceFile`, short `/sf`). **`BlockOnPossibleDataLoss`** defaults to True and stops the deployment if a change could lose data; **`DropObjectsNotInSource`** defaults to False (when True, use `DoNotDropObjectTypes` to protect types such as users or permissions). `/AccessToken` supports token authentication.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`Deploy`** isn't an action. **`Import`** loads a `.bacpac` into a *new* database, so it can't update an existing one.\n- **`InputFile`** isn't a parameter. **`TargetFile`** is the file Extract or Export *writes*.\n- **`Warn`** isn't a value. `BlockOnPossibleDataLoss` is a boolean: `True` stops the deployment, `False` lets the column shrink or the table drop.\n- **`Never`** isn't a value either. `DropObjectsNotInSource=True` drops everything in the database that isn't in the `.dacpac`, such as a `dbo.Report_Temp` table an analyst created. Keep it `False` unless the project really owns everything, and then protect object types with `/p:DoNotDropObjectTypes=Users;Logins;RoleMembership`.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage publish](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-publish)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"sqlpackage /Action:[[0]] \\\n  /[[1]]:bin/Release/Db.dacpac \\\n  /TargetConnectionString:\"$CONN\" \\\n  /p:BlockOnPossibleDataLoss=[[2]] \\\n  /p:DropObjectsNotInSource=[[3]] \\\n  /v:EnvironmentName=production","options":[["Deploy","Import","Publish"],["InputFile","SourceFile","TargetFile"],["False","True","Warn"],["False","Never","True"]],"answer":["Publish","SourceFile","True","False"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.2-2","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.2 · Publish and its properties","scenario":"","stem":"Match each Publish property to its effect.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Other properties: `CreateNewDatabase` (drop and re-create), `ScriptDatabaseOptions` (default True), `IgnorePermissions`, `IgnoreRoleMembership`, `IncludeTransactionalScripts`, `CommandTimeout` (default 60 seconds) and `TreatVerificationErrorsAsWarnings`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`GenerateSmartDefaults=True`:** adding `IsActive bit NOT NULL` to a `Customer` table with rows would fail. With smart defaults, the deployment adds a temporary default of `0` to fill the existing rows.\n- **`IgnoreColumnOrder=True`:** if you add a column in the middle of the `CREATE TABLE` in the project, the deployment adds it at the end of the existing table instead of copying the table into a new one.\n- **`BackupDatabaseBeforeChanges=True`:** on SQL Server, runs a `BACKUP DATABASE` before the first change. Azure SQL Database relies on its automatic backups and point-in-time restore instead.\n- **`ExcludeObjectTypes=Users;Permissions`:** the dev team's users in the project don't get created in production, and production's `GRANT`s aren't revoked.\n- **Drops the database and re-creates it** is `CreateNewDatabase=True` (it has its own question). Fine for a throwaway test database, and destroys all data anywhere else.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage publish](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-publish)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`GenerateSmartDefaults=True`","`IgnoreColumnOrder=True`","`BackupDatabaseBeforeChanges=True`","`ExcludeObjectTypes=Users;Permissions`"],"values":["Backs up the database before deploying (not for Azure SQL Database)","Doesn't rebuild tables just because column order differs","Drops the database and re-creates it","Ignores those object types during deployment","Supplies a default when adding a NOT NULL column to a table that has data"],"answer":["Supplies a default when adding a NOT NULL column to a table that has data","Doesn't rebuild tables just because column order differs","Backs up the database before deploying (not for Azure SQL Database)","Ignores those object types during deployment"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.2-3","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.2 · Publish and its properties","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Publish.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Publish creates or incrementally updates the database. `DropObjectsNotInSource` is False by default, so objects added outside the project survive unless you opt in. Token authentication avoids passwords in pipelines.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Creates the database if it's missing:** publishing to `Database=Sales_PR123` on a test server creates `Sales_PR123` with the whole schema. That's handy for a database per pull request.\n- **`CreateNewDatabase=True`:** every publish starts from an empty database. Use it for integration tests that need a clean schema, never for production.\n- **`DropObjectsNotInSource` defaults to True** is false. By default, an index a DBA added in production survives a publish of a project that doesn't have it.\n- **`/AccessToken`:** in a pipeline, `TOKEN=$(az account get-access-token --resource https://database.windows.net/ --query accessToken -o tsv)`, then `sqlpackage /Action:Publish ... /AccessToken:$TOKEN`. There's no password in the connection string.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage publish](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-publish)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["If the database doesn't exist, Publish creates it.","`CreateNewDatabase=True` drops and re-creates the target database.","`DropObjectsNotInSource` defaults to True.","`/AccessToken` (`/at`) lets you authenticate with a token, such as one from a managed identity."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.2-4","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.2 · Publish and its properties","scenario":"","stem":"A publish fails with timeouts on a large table rebuild. Which properties tune SqlPackage's timeouts?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Timeouts are tuned with those three properties. For any SqlPackage problem, capture logs with **`/DiagnosticsFile:<file>`**. Export and Extract also buffer table data in a temp directory (`TempDirectoryForTableData`), and large schemas need a lot of memory (`/p:Storage=File` helps).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **The three timeouts:** for a table rebuild that takes 20 minutes, `/p:CommandTimeout=1800` or `/p:LongRunningCommandTimeout=0` (wait indefinitely) stops the publish from giving up after 60 seconds.\n- **`BlockOnPossibleDataLoss`** decides whether a change that could lose data runs at all. It has nothing to do with time.\n- **`ScriptDatabaseOptions`** decides whether database options such as `ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION` are part of the deployment.\n- **`/v:Timeout=600`** only defines a SQLCMD variable named `Timeout` for your scripts. SqlPackage itself ignores it.\n\nWhen you don't know what's slow, add `/DiagnosticsFile:publish.log` to see each step and its timing.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage publish](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-publish)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`/p:CommandTimeout` (default 60), `/p:DatabaseLockTimeout` (default 60) and `/p:LongRunningCommandTimeout` (default 0 = indefinitely)"},{"key":"B","text":"`/p:BlockOnPossibleDataLoss`"},{"key":"C","text":"`/p:ScriptDatabaseOptions`"},{"key":"D","text":"`/v:Timeout=600`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-5.3-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.3 · Publish profiles","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about publish profiles.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Profiles store reusable deployment settings, so you can keep one per environment (dev, test, prod). Avoid storing secrets such as passwords in them; pass the connection string or an access token from your pipeline's secrets instead.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`.publish.xml`:** for example, `Sales.prod.publish.xml`:\n\n  ```xml\n  <Project ToolsVersion=\"Current\" xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003\">\n    <PropertyGroup>\n      <TargetDatabaseName>Sales</TargetDatabaseName>\n      <BlockOnPossibleDataLoss>True</BlockOnPossibleDataLoss>\n      <ExcludeObjectTypes>Users;Permissions</ExcludeObjectTypes>\n    </PropertyGroup>\n    <ItemGroup>\n      <SqlCmdVariable Include=\"EnvironmentName\"><Value>production</Value></SqlCmdVariable>\n    </ItemGroup>\n  </Project>\n  ```\n\n- **`/Profile:`:** `sqlpackage /Action:Publish /SourceFile:Db.dacpac /Profile:Sales.prod.publish.xml /TargetConnectionString:\"$CONN\"`. The pipeline supplies the connection string, so no secret is in the file.\n- **Only one per project** is false. Keep `Sales.dev.publish.xml`, `Sales.test.publish.xml` and `Sales.prod.publish.xml` side by side, each with its own options and variables.\n\n**Source:** [publish profiles](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/publish-profiles)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A publish profile is an XML file with the `.publish.xml` extension.","It can hold the target connection, the target database name, deployment options and SQLCMD variable values.","SqlPackage uses one with `/Profile:<file>`.","You can only have one publish profile per project."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.4-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.4 · Extract, Export, Import and Script","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the SqlPackage action.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Extract** creates a `.dacpac` (add `/p:ExtractAllTableData=true` for data; `ExtractTarget` can write SQL files or a project instead). **Export** writes a `.bacpac` (schema plus data, limited to the Azure SQL Database surface area), and **Import** loads a `.bacpac` into a **new** database.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Extract:** `sqlpackage /Action:Extract /SourceConnectionString:\"...\" /TargetFile:Sales.dacpac` (add `/p:ExtractAllTableData=true` to include rows).\n- **Export:** `sqlpackage /Action:Export /SourceConnectionString:\"...azure...\" /TargetFile:Sales.bacpac`, then copy the file to the on-premises server.\n- **Import:** `sqlpackage /Action:Import /SourceFile:Sales.bacpac /TargetConnectionString:\"Server=onprem;Database=Sales;...\"` creates the database there.\n- **Extract to a project:** `/p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject` with `/TargetFile:Sales` writes a `.sqlproj` and one `.sql` file per object.\n- **Publish** (it has its own questions) *updates* a database from a `.dacpac`. It doesn't produce a file or move data.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage extract](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-extract) · [sqlpackage export](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-export) · [sqlpackage import](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-import) · [sqlpackage script](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-script) · [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/data-tier-applications/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Save a database's schema as a `.dacpac` (optionally with table data)","Move schema and data from Azure SQL to SQL Server as a file","Create a new database from a `.bacpac`","Turn an existing database into SQL project files"],"values":["Export","Extract","Extract with /p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject","Import","Publish"],"answer":["Extract","Export","Import","Extract with /p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.4-2","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.4 · Extract, Export, Import and Script","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Export and Import.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"A `.bacpac` carries schema and data for portability (for example Azure SQL Database to SQL Server). Import needs a **new or empty** database. Its model is limited to the Azure SQL Database surface area.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **All tables by default:** an export of a 40 GB database writes a `.bacpac` with every row of every table, and can take a long time.\n- **`/p:TableData`:** `/p:TableData=dbo.Country /p:TableData=dbo.OrderStatus` (repeatable) exports the whole schema but only those two tables' rows. That makes a small copy for a developer.\n- **Import into a database with user objects** is false. It fails with *Data cannot be imported into target because it contains one or more user objects. Import should be performed against a new, empty database.*\n- **Service tier on import:** `/p:DatabaseEdition=Hyperscale /p:DatabaseServiceObjective=HS_Gen5_4` creates the new Azure SQL database at that size directly, so you don't have to scale it afterwards.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage extract](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-extract) · [sqlpackage export](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-export) · [sqlpackage import](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-import) · [sqlpackage script](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-script) · [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/data-tier-applications/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Export includes data for all tables by default.","`/p:TableData` can limit the export to specific tables.","Import can load a `.bacpac` into a database that already has user objects.","When importing into Azure SQL Database, `/p:DatabaseEdition` and `/p:DatabaseServiceObjective` set the tier, such as Hyperscale or S1."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-5.4-3","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 5.4 · Extract, Export, Import and Script","scenario":"","stem":"You need a `.dacpac` of a production database's schema to start a SQL project, with no data. Which command fits?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Extract** produces a `.dacpac` (schema only by default; `ExtractAllTableData=true` includes data). `/p:VerifyExtraction=true` checks the result, and `/p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject` writes project files instead. Export always creates a `.bacpac`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Extract with `ExtractAllTableData=false`:** a schema-only `prod.dacpac`. Add `/p:ExtractTarget=SqlProject` to get the project files straight away.\n- **Export** always writes a `.bacpac` with data, whatever you name the file, and this command doesn't even name a source database.\n- **Publish** *deploys* `prod.dacpac` to a target, which is the opposite direction.\n- **Import** creates a new database from a `.bacpac`.\n\n**Source:** [sqlpackage extract](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-extract) · [sqlpackage export](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-export) · [sqlpackage import](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-import) · [sqlpackage script](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-script) · [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/data-tier-applications/overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`sqlpackage /Action:Extract /TargetFile:prod.dacpac /SourceConnectionString:\"...\" /p:ExtractAllTableData=false`"},{"key":"B","text":"`sqlpackage /Action:Export /TargetFile:prod.dacpac`"},{"key":"C","text":"`sqlpackage /Action:Publish /SourceFile:prod.dacpac`"},{"key":"D","text":"`sqlpackage /Action:Import /SourceFile:prod.bacpac`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-6.1-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 6.1 · Build and deploy stages in a pipeline","scenario":"","stem":"A CI/CD pipeline deploys a SQL project. Which practices do the docs describe? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Build once (with code analysis if you like), keep the `.dacpac` as an artifact, and deploy that same artifact through environments with **approvals and gates** (GitHub environments or Azure DevOps). Windows hosted runners include SqlPackage; on Linux and macOS install it with `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.SqlPackage` (after `actions/setup-dotnet`).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nA GitHub Actions outline:\n\n```yaml\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: dotnet build Db/Db.sqlproj -c Release\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n        with: { name: dacpac, path: Db/bin/Release/Db.dacpac }\n  deploy-prod:\n    needs: build\n    environment: production          # required reviewers approve here\n    runs-on: windows-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/download-artifact@v4\n        with: { name: dacpac }\n      - run: sqlpackage /Action:DeployReport /SourceFile:Db.dacpac /TargetConnectionString:\"${{ secrets.SQL_CONN }}\" /OutputPath:report.xml\n      - run: sqlpackage /Action:Publish /SourceFile:Db.dacpac /TargetConnectionString:\"${{ secrets.SQL_CONN }}\"\n```\n\n- **Rebuild in every stage** is false. Rebuilding could pick up a different commit or SDK version, so the `.dacpac` that reaches production might not be the one you tested. Build once and promote that same file.\n\n**Source:** [sql projects automation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-automation) · [sqlpackage pipelines](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-pipelines)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Build with `dotnet build` and publish the `.dacpac` as a pipeline artifact"},{"key":"B","text":"Deploy the artifact in a later job, after environment approvals for production"},{"key":"C","text":"Generate a deployment script or report for review before deploying to sensitive environments"},{"key":"D","text":"Rebuild the project separately in every deployment stage"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"sqlproj-6.1-2","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 6.1 · Build and deploy stages in a pipeline","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about running SqlPackage in pipelines.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Install the .NET SDK first (`actions/setup-dotnet` or the `UseDotNet` task) if the runner lacks it. Use a **self-hosted** runner when the target database isn't reachable from Microsoft-hosted agents (for example, private networking) or you need more control. Log `SqlPackage /Version` to help troubleshooting.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Preinstalled on Windows runners:** a `windows-latest` job can call `sqlpackage` with no install step.\n- **Linux install:** after `actions/setup-dotnet`, run `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.SqlPackage`.\n- **Pinning:** `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.SqlPackage --version <version>` keeps every run on the same version until you upgrade on purpose.\n- **Never need self-hosted runners** is false. If the database only has a private endpoint, a Microsoft-hosted runner on the internet can't reach it. A self-hosted runner in the same VNet can.\n\n**Source:** [sql projects automation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-automation) · [sqlpackage pipelines](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-pipelines)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["SqlPackage is preinstalled on Microsoft-hosted Windows runners.","On Linux runners, install it with `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.SqlPackage`.","You can pin a version with `--version <version>`.","Self-hosted runners are never needed."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-6.2-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 6.2 · GitHub Actions for Azure SQL","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the GitHub Actions workflow that signs in to Azure with OpenID Connect and publishes a `.dacpac` to Azure SQL.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Workflows live in `.github/workflows/`. **`azure/login`** signs in with OIDC (a federated credential on an app registration or user-assigned managed identity, so no secret) or with a service principal's `AZURE_CREDENTIALS` JSON. **`azure/sql-action`** deploys the `.dacpac` (`action: publish`) using a connection string stored as a GitHub secret. Use environment secrets with required reviewers for extra protection.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`AZURE_CREDENTIALS`** is the secret for the *service principal* option (it has its own question). `azure/login` reads it with `creds: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CREDENTIALS }}`. This workflow uses OIDC with `client-id`, `tenant-id` and `subscription-id`, and `connection-string` takes the SQL connection string secret.\n- **`azure/sqlpackage@v1`** isn't the action the docs use. `azure/sql-action` is the one that deploys a `.dacpac` (it runs SqlPackage for you).\n- **`deploy`** isn't one of the action's values. With a `.dacpac`, `action` takes a SqlPackage action such as `publish`, `script`, `deployreport` or `driftreport`. For example, `action: 'script'` writes the T-SQL without changing the database.\n\nFor OIDC, the job also needs `permissions: id-token: write`, so it can ask GitHub for the token that `azure/login` exchanges.\n\n**Source:** [connect github actions sql db](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/connect-github-actions-sql-db)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"# .github/workflows/sql.yml\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: windows-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: [[0]]\n        with:\n          client-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}\n          tenant-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}\n          subscription-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}\n      - uses: [[1]]\n        with:\n          connection-string: ${{ secrets.[[2]] }}\n          path: './Database.dacpac'\n          action: '[[3]]'","values":["AZURE_CREDENTIALS","AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING","azure/login@v2","azure/sql-action@v2.3","azure/sqlpackage@v1","deploy","publish"],"answer":["azure/login@v2","azure/sql-action@v2.3","AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING","publish"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-6.2-2","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 6.2 · GitHub Actions for Azure SQL","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about securing the GitHub Actions deployment.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"OIDC uses a **federated identity credential** that trusts tokens from your repository; you store only the client, tenant and subscription IDs. Keep connection strings in secrets (preferably **environment** secrets with required reviewers in public repositories), never in the repository.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **No client secret with OIDC:** the federated credential trusts, for example, subject `repo:contoso/sales-db:environment:production`. Only jobs in that environment of that repository get a token, and there's nothing in GitHub that could leak.\n- **Secrets, not values in the workflow:** `${{ secrets.SQL_CONN }}` is masked in logs as `***`. A value typed into the YAML is visible to everyone who can read the repository, and stays in its history.\n- **Reviewers for environment secrets:** with `environment: production` on the deploy job and required reviewers on that environment, the job waits for approval before it can read `SQL_CONN`.\n- **Commit the connection string** is false. Every fork, clone and old commit would have it, so treat it as leaked and rotate the password if it ever happens.\n\n**Source:** [connect github actions sql db](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/connect-github-actions-sql-db)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["With OpenID Connect, you don't store a client secret in GitHub.","Microsoft recommends GitHub secrets rather than putting values directly in the workflow.","Environment secrets can require a reviewer's approval before a job can read them.","The connection string should be committed in the workflow file so the runner can read it."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"sqlproj-6.2-3","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"sqlproj","sourceLabel":"SQL projects 6.2 · GitHub Actions for Azure SQL","scenario":"","stem":"You use a service principal with a client secret instead of OpenID Connect. What do you store in the `AZURE_CREDENTIALS` secret?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"For the service principal option, `azure/login` takes `creds: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CREDENTIALS }}`, the whole JSON from the CLI. With **OIDC**, you instead pass `client-id`, `tenant-id` and `subscription-id` and keep no secret. OIDC is the more secure choice.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **The CLI's JSON:** create it with\n\n  ```bash\n  az ad sp create-for-rbac --name gh-sales-deploy --role contributor \\\n    --scopes /subscriptions/<id>/resourceGroups/rg-sales --json-auth\n  ```\n\n  and paste the whole output (`clientId`, `clientSecret`, `subscriptionId`, `tenantId` and endpoints) into `AZURE_CREDENTIALS`.\n- **Only the client secret:** `azure/login` also needs to know which app, tenant and subscription the secret belongs to, so the login fails.\n- **The SQL connection string** is a different secret that `azure/sql-action` uses, not a credential for signing in to Azure.\n- **An SSH key** is for Git or VMs. Azure sign-in for a service principal uses a secret or a certificate.\n\n**Source:** [connect github actions sql db](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/connect-github-actions-sql-db)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The JSON output from the Azure CLI that contains the client ID, client secret, subscription ID and tenant ID"},{"key":"B","text":"Only the client secret"},{"key":"C","text":"The SQL connection string"},{"key":"D","text":"An SSH key"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""}]};
